  "doc.title": "How a Network Card Moves Packets · a long essay you can play with",
  "doc.desc": "A 400G network card receives more than thirty million packets a second, about thirty nanoseconds each, less than one memory access. How does it move them from the fibre into the memory of the right program with the CPU hardly involved? Ten hands-on models: one Ethernet frame and its check sequence, the time per packet at line rate, why descriptors are fetched ahead, whether PCIe can carry 400G, the Toeplitz hash behind RSS, how a few big flows skew the queues, how much CPU segmentation and receive offloads save, why DDIO leaks, how many bytes belong in the transmit ring, how many cores the kernel stack, XDP and DPDK need, and ECMP versus packet spraying in a training cluster.",
