  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How a Network Card Moves Packets",
  "hero.sub": "A 400G network card receives more than thirty million packets a second, about thirty nanoseconds each, less than one memory access.<br>How does it move them from the fibre into the memory of the right program, with the CPU hardly involved?",
  "hero.lead": "We follow one packet from start to finish. First the parts inside the card, what an Ethernet frame looks like, and how much time each packet gets at line rate. Then the receive path: the card gets the addresses of empty buffers in advance, writes the packet into memory by DMA itself, and then tells the driver. How many extra bytes PCIe must pay for each packet. How dozens of queues use a hash to hand packets to dozens of cores. Which jobs the card can take over (checksums, segmentation, merging). Whether a packet is written into the cache or into memory. Why the transmit ring must never be filled to the brim. And three bolder routes: XDP, which handles packets in the driver; DPDK, which bypasses the kernel altogether; and RDMA, which writes straight into another machine's memory. Last come the cards in AI training clusters, and the frontier as of November 2026. <strong>Every model is a scaled-down version of something real</strong>: Ethernet frame checks computed to IEEE 802.3, Toeplitz hashes checked against Microsoft's published test vectors, PCIe bytes counted from the formulas in the papers, a cache simulation with LRU replacement, and a transmit queue advanced step by step in time.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The rules come from primary sources (public summaries of the IEEE 802.3 frame format, Microsoft's RSS documentation and test vectors, the Linux kernel documentation on scaling, segmentation offloads, XDP and AF_XDP, and the NVIDIA NIC manuals). The measurements come from several papers (Neugebauer et al., SIGCOMM 2018; Høiland-Jørgensen et al., CoNEXT 2018; Farshin et al., USENIX ATC 2020; Cai et al., SIGCOMM 2021), with the source and date given for each. <strong>Measurements from different years and different machines can only be compared by order of magnitude</strong>. Values marked “tuned parameter” were chosen so the models could compute; the text says how far the conclusion moves when each one doubles.",

  "toc.ch1": "What is inside a network card",
